     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Rhythm control (AF):"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " class Ia, Ic or III drugs can convert AF to sinus rhythm; except amiodarone and sotalol, which also slow the rate, don't use them until the rate = controlled; oral forms also maintain sinus rhythm (amiodarone most effective, but more adverse events). In flutter, class Ia/Ic can allow 1:1 conduction, so control the rate first w BB or NDHP CCB."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "AF/AFL rate control:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " the least effective rate-control drug, but may be preferred when HF = present; w antiarrhythmics for flutter, it = one of the AV-nodal drugs given alongside."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "B4 elective cardioversion of AF, consider reducing or holding digoxin 1–2 days; delay elective cardioversion if digoxin toxicity = sus."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Dose:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " individualized by age, lean body wt, renal funct and other drugs; if loading, give ½ the total load first, then ¼ every 6–8 h twice; lower maintenance doses in renal impairment."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Serum level:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " <0.5 ng/mL loses efficacy and >2 ng/mL adds toxicity w.o benefit; draw just b4 the next dose or ≥6 h after the last dose, and don't Δ the dose on one isolated level."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Toxicity:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " N/V, visual chgs and arrhythmias; ↔ VT, nonparoxysmal junctional tachy in AF and hyperkalemia are serious signs; hypokalemia or hypomagnesemia allows toxicity @ lower levels. Stop digoxin, monitor, keep K 4.0–5.5; Digoxin Immune Fab for life-threatening arrhythmias or hyperkalemia; atropine for brady/♡ block; ⍉removed by dialysis."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Counsel:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " report N/V, persistent diarrhea, confusion, weakness or visual chgs (blurred or green-yellow vision, halos), which can mean the dose = too ↑."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
